.TH RSA2SSH 1
.SH NAME
rsa2ssh \- convert a Plan 9 RSA key to OpenSSH authorized_keys format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/rsa2ssh
[
.B -c
.I comment
]
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rsa2ssh
is a real, structural translation of Plan 9's own
.B auth/rsa2ssh
(from
.BR sys/src/cmd/auth/rsa2ssh.c ,
9front), compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu .
It lives under
.B appl/cmd/auth
alongside its sibling
.IR rsagen (1),
not under
.BR p9/cmd ,
since its whole job is converting the SAME "key ..." attribute-line
format this tree's own keyring/factotum ecosystem already uses (see
.IR rsagen (1)'s
own output) into the "ssh-rsa ..." line an OpenSSH
.B authorized_keys
file expects - a real Inferno-native tie-in, not a host-userspace
filter with none.
.PP
.I Rsa2ssh
reads a Plan 9 RSA public or private key -
.RB ( proto=rsa ,
.B ek
and
.B n
attributes at minimum) - from
.I file
or standard input, and writes one line:
.B ssh-rsa
followed by the key's standard base64-encoded SSH wire encoding,
followed by
.IR comment .
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate a fresh key and configure a remote Unix system to allow use
of that key for logins (the same recipe as Plan 9's own
.IR rsa (8)):
.IP
.EX
auth/rsagen -t 'service=ssh role=client' >key
auth/rsa2ssh key | ssh unix 'cat >>.ssh/authorized_keys'
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B appl/cmd/auth/rsa2ssh.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/auth/rsa2ssh.c ,
plus the shared
.BR put4 / putn / putmp2
wire-format helpers from the same directory's
.BR rsa2any.c ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rsa2sshtest (1),
.IR ssh2rsa (1),
.IR rsagen (1)
.SH BUGS
Only the one real behavior
.BR rsa2any.c 's
.B getrsakey()
needs is translated (read a "key ..." line, require
.BR proto=rsa ,
pull
.B ek
/
.B n
out as hex) - not its general quoted-attribute-value parsing (Plan 9's
.B _parseattr()
convention), since every field this command reads is always a plain
hex or decimal token with no spaces or quotes in every key line this
tree's own tools produce; not its "missing/wrong size: warn and
correct" self-healing logic either, since a key missing a usable
.B size
attribute is not something
.IR rsagen (1) 's
own output ever produces.
